<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Project ToolsVersion="4.0" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/developer/msbuild/2003">

	<!--
Author: mingkuang
Modification date: 2018-08-14


VC-LTL automatic loading configuration, it is recommended that you copy this file to your project and use it. 
This file can automatically identify whether there is VC-LTL in the current environment and apply it automatically.

Instructions:
  1. Select all Release configurations in the Property Manager, then right-click and select "Add Existing Property Sheet".
  2. Make sure that all dependent static libraries are also recompiled with VC-LTL.


VC-LTL default search order
  1. The directory where VC-LTL.props is located, i.e. $(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)
  2. The current project root directory, i.e. $(ProjectDir)VC-LTL
  3. The former solution root directory, i.e. $(SolutionDir)VC-LTL
  4. The current project parent directory, i.e. $(ProjectDir)..\VC-LTL
  5. The current solution parent directory, i.e. $(SolutionDir)..\VC-LTL
  6. Registry HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Code\VC-LTL@Root

Just put the VC-LTL in one of these locations, and the VC-LTL will be automatically referenced.

If you are not satisfied with the default search order, you can modify this file. You can also directly specify the $(VC_LTL_Root) macro to load VC-LTL more arbitrarily.

-->

	<!--VC-LTL settings-->
	<PropertyGroup>
		<!--<DisableAdvancedSupport>true</DisableAdvancedSupport>-->
		<!--<SupportWinXP>true</SupportWinXP>-->
	</PropertyGroup>

	<PropertyGroup>
		<!--Shared.props file root directory exists VC-LTL? -->
		<VC_LTL_Root Condition=" '$(VC_LTL_Root)'=='' ">$(SolutionDir)packages\VC-LTL.5.1.1\build\native</VC_LTL_Root>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ImportGroup Condition=" '$(VC_LTL_Root)'!=''">
		<Import Project="$(VC_LTL_Root)\Config\config.props" Condition="Exists('$(VC_LTL_Root)\Config\config.props')"/>
		<Import Project="$(VC_LTL_Root)\ltlvcrt.props" Condition="(!Exists('$(VC_LTL_Root)\Config\config.props')) And (Exists('$(VC_LTL_Root)\ltlvcrt.props'))"/>
	</ImportGroup>

	<!--
	Shell addition, below this line. Not part of the vendor file; re-apply it if
	this file is ever refreshed from upstream VC-LTL.

	Everything above decides whether VC-LTL applies by testing whether a file
	exists, and says nothing when it does not. That made the CRT the product
	ships a side effect of whether src\packages happened to be restored, with
	both halves silent: CI runs `nuget restore` and ships a build importing
	msvcrt.dll, while build.ps1 ran no restore at all and produced a static-CRT
	binary 361 KB larger with 13 imports instead of 14. Nothing in either log
	said which one you had, and the difference was measured, not guessed
	(dumpbin /imports, x64, 2026-08-27).

	config.props sets SupportLTL as it is imported, so an unset SupportLTL is an
	exact test for "the import above did not happen". Building without VC-LTL
	stays possible - offline, or to bisect a CRT-sensitive bug - but it has to
	be asked for, and it still says so.
	-->
	<Target Name="ShellVerifyVCLTLApplied" BeforeTargets="PrepareForBuild">
		<Error Code="SHELL9000"
			   Condition="'$(SupportLTL)'!='true' And '$(SupportLTL)'!='ucrt' And '$(ShellAllowNoVCLTL)'!='true'"
			   Text="VC-LTL did not apply to $(MSBuildProjectName) ($(Platform)), so this build would link the static CRT and would not be what CI ships. Expected the package under $(VC_LTL_Root). Restore it with: msbuild -t:restore -p:RestorePackagesConfig=true src\Shell.sln  (build.ps1 does this for you). To build without VC-LTL deliberately, pass /p:ShellAllowNoVCLTL=true." />
		<Warning Code="SHELL9001"
				 Condition="'$(SupportLTL)'!='true' And '$(SupportLTL)'!='ucrt' And '$(ShellAllowNoVCLTL)'=='true'"
				 Text="$(MSBuildProjectName) ($(Platform)) is being built without VC-LTL by request: the static CRT is linked and this binary is not what CI ships." />
	</Target>
</Project>